Z||000014||bHooper||06-30-2000||12:21 PM||||BM... very nice looking car!  I cant wait to have my car painted, but that is the last thing planned, so it will be a while.<P>Enjoy!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000015||BOILERMAKER||06-30-2000||07:55 PM||||For those who want to know exactly what I did (maybe I should keep this a secret  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  I can really only speak from the body work aspect of the job.<P>Sanding was the biggest part of the job.  I started with 220 grit for most of the job.  The nose actually took 80 grit to get down to the base of the pits (yeah, those were some healthy chips).  As far as wet sanding, I usually don't do that.  I was told a long time ago that a person can do that if they are wanting to show the car, but honestly I'm driving this car, so what is the use?<P>The same person also told me that a good painter will be able to "fill" in 220 and 320 grit marks (they aren't that bad) and for those who don't believe me, I welcome you to take a look at my car.<P>I didn't have a lot of filling to do.  We did some (dad filled and I use the DA *Dual Action Sander*) and exactly what we used I will have to check into. <P>AS far as what the paint was, I will have to ask THE MAN about that one.  I know we used BASE COAT/CLEAR COAT, and we didn't primer it.  Maybe we should have, but we didn't on this car.  Now, on the black one, that is another story.<P>I'll try to answer any more question you have.  Keep them coming.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Eric||07-06-2000||11:53 AM||||Okay I've got one for you, BOILERMAKER.  What grit did you start with while using the DA on the bumpers?  Also, did you find the back bumper needed less sanding than the front (I see the black base coat is still on the back)?  Is there any other bumper sanding advice you can offer up, thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000017||BOILERMAKER||07-06-2000||02:17 PM||||I used 80 grit on the front fascia due to it being so pitted.  It was very cracked too, so I had to use an aggressive grit on the front and follow up with 220 and then a light "once-over" with 320.<P>The rear I strictly used 220 on because it wasn't as bad as the front.  That's why you see the paint on the back and more of an effort on the front.<P>Mainly you are looking for a smooth transition between layers of paint down to where the base of the flaw you are trying to eliminate.  It's a focus/broad/focus/broad sanding technique that is used to get these flaws out of the paint.  <P>Once you think you have the flaw out of the paint you should really get some kind of cleaner to get all of the dust off of the area you are working on.  Something that glosses and gives a shine to the area works best because it gives you an idea of what the paint on the surface will look like.<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>86 Black 3.1 GT<BR>87 Gold GT||||reg||1
